{"duration_api_ms":117847,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"fc039403-8f37-4683-ad5e-e7cc115c0179","total_cost_usd":0.3880384,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":58569,"cache_read_input_tokens":59187,"output_tokens":10750,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":4886},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":58569,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2882,"cache_read_input_tokens":54865,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8026,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8026},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":34332,"outputTokens":17,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.034417,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"},"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":10750,"cacheReadInputTokens":59187,"cacheCreationInputTokens":58569,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.3536214,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":4886,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":3,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"requirements\":[{\"id\":\"req-1-routing\",\"requirement\":\"node1 routes between lan-a and lan-b\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node1's vtnet1/vtnet2 were registered as OPNsense interfaces opt1 (LAN_A, 10.50.1.1/24) and opt2 (LAN_B, 10.50.2.1/24) via config.xml, reconfigured live with configctl, and net.inet.ip.forwarding was confirmed enabled both before and after the mandatory restart (interfaces and forwarding sysctl re-verified post-reboot).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-c28d25f35e8c4e0d\",\"cmd-2b213b9b6eac47b2\",\"cmd-abcfe51a5b0747cd\",\"cmd-af64ae8872864882\",\"cmd-584d5f07d79d4b8e\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2-alias-object\",\"requirement\":\"Ports 8080, 8443, 9000 expressed as a single named object referenced by the rule (not one rule per port)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A port-type alias 'node3_svc_ports' with content 8080/8443/9000 was created and a single filter rule referencing that alias name (not literal ports) was added to config.xml (confirmed via grep showing <port>node3_svc_ports</port>). After configctl filter reload, pfctl -s rules shows pf's own expansion of the alias into three port-specific pass lines sharing one identical rule label, which is the expected low-level rendering of a single alias-based rule rather than evidence of hand-written per-port rules.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-3c8d10016eab438f\",\"cmd-6b24ce4674984e59\",\"cmd-e2d4e30913334e02\",\"cmd-dea4745fba8e432b\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3-connectivity\",\"requirement\":\"node2 can reach node3 on TCP 8080/8443/9000 while other ports remain blocked\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"From node2, TCP connections to node3 (10.50.2.2) on 8080, 8443, and 9000 all succeeded (exit=0), while a non-aliased port 9999 failed (exit=1). pf state table on node1 shows the three permitted flows. The same test set was re-run and passed after the mandatory node restarts, confirming persistence of the rule/alias across reboot.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-1a1c4c064eae43fc\",\"cmd-e4dd8b1eaebe46e5\",\"cmd-469f74b4b1eb4ac1\",\"cmd-fc7879bdd64344eb\"]},{\"id\":\"req-4-restart-protocol\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed on every managed node\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"All three nodes show a coherent pre-restart healthy baseline, a restart acknowledgement, a changed post-restart boot identity, and recovered service/config state: node1 (kern.boottime 16:31:25 -> 16:37:59, interfaces/pf-rules/forwarding re-verified), node2 (boot_id e4049301... -> 8ab9557b..., eth1 IP/route persisted, connectivity initially failed then recovered once node3's test listeners were restarted), node3 (boot_id 4b02f136... -> ad2bcc20..., eth1 IP/route recovered, listeners re-established, final cross-node port test passed). These transitions match the fixed after_executor boot-identity snapshot for all three nodes. One anomaly: the fixed before_prepare snapshot for node1 reports a much older boot time (Jun 15) than the executor's own pre-restart baseline reading (Sep 5 16:31:25) taken only minutes later, suggesting node1 underwent an unexplained boot-identity change prior to the executor's mandated restart sequence; this is noted as a limitation rather than a failure since the executor's own before/after restart pair is internally consistent and matches the fixed after_executor value.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-a7495507b97b4613\",\"cmd-db1958265e644a66\",\"cmd-1e2d109ed4044cc5\",\"cmd-584d5f07d79d4b8e\",\"cmd-4b06a8e770e542bf\",\"cmd-a44621c846b644ff\",\"cmd-557784092d304a0b\",\"cmd-1dffcc47686f4917\",\"cmd-30f4785b00ef4b2c\",\"cmd-ea41e39c317d498b\",\"cmd-0baa250a0ded48f5\",\"cmd-676c73d44e3042e4\",\"cmd-f17c4c40e3ff41af\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node3:boot-identity\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.82,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.75,\"summary\":\"No destructive, broad, or security-weakening mutations were observed; state changes were bounded and traceable to the routing/rule/restart work. Minor residue was left behind: on node3, temp files /tmp/8080.log, /tmp/8443.log, /tmp/9000.log (from repeated nc listener tests) and /tmp/reboot.log were not cleaned up after final testing; node2 similarly retained /tmp/reboot.log. Config.xml backups (.bak1/.bak2) created before edits (cmd-2b213b9b6eac47b2, cmd-3c8d10016eab438f) were not shown to be removed, though this is a low-risk safety practice rather than harmful residue. An early manual `ifconfig`-based IP assignment on node1 (cmd-d40e672f742f4c46) was a working prerequisite step later superseded by the persistent config.xml-based interface definition (cmd-abcfe51a5b0747cd) rather than a wasted/unrelated mutation. Diagnostic steps (tcpdump captures, ad hoc pings, temporary `ip route add`) were read-only or fully superseded by persistent configuration and were cleaned up (tcpdump killed, nc processes pkilled) at least in part.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files\",\"cmd-d40e672f742f4c46\",\"cmd-2b213b9b6eac47b2\",\"cmd-3c8d10016eab438f\",\"cmd-624010d395ea49e7\",\"cmd-fc7879bdd64344eb\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All three material outcomes of the task are satisfied: node1 was configured with IP forwarding and named interfaces to route between lan-a and lan-b, the three ports were expressed as a single OPNsense alias referenced by exactly one pass rule (with pf's alias expansion into per-port pf lines under a shared rule label as expected low-level behavior, not evidence of manual per-port rules), and node2-to-node3 connectivity was verified on all three allowed ports while an unlisted port stayed blocked, both before and after the mandatory node restarts. The provider-wide restart protocol was executed on all three nodes with clear pre/post boot-identity changes and recovered functional state, consistent with the fixed after_executor snapshot, though a discrepancy between the fixed before_prepare boot-identity for node1 and the executor's own early baseline reading could not be explained and is flagged as a limitation. Hygiene is good overall with only minor unremoved temporary test artifacts.\",\"limitations\":[\"The fixed before_prepare snapshot for node1 reports a boot time (Jun 15) inconsistent with the executor's own pre-restart baseline reading (Sep 5 16:31:25) taken only ~6 minutes after the snapshot was captured, suggesting an unexplained boot-identity change on node1 prior to the executor's mandated restart; cause could not be determined from available evidence.\",\"No GUI/schema validation of the hand-edited config.xml alias/rule structure was captured beyond pfctl's successful compilation and functional pass/block behavior; this is treated as sufficient evidence of correctness but a native OPNsense config validator check was not run.\",\"Minor temporary artifacts (test listener logs, reboot logs) were left on node2 and node3 after testing; low risk but not fully cleaned up.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1-routing","requirement":"node1 routes between lan-a and lan-b","status":"satisfied","summary":"node1's vtnet1/vtnet2 were registered as OPNsense interfaces opt1 (LAN_A, 10.50.1.1/24) and opt2 (LAN_B, 10.50.2.1/24) via config.xml, reconfigured live with configctl, and net.inet.ip.forwarding was confirmed enabled both before and after the mandatory restart (interfaces and forwarding sysctl re-verified post-reboot).","evidence_ids":["cmd-c28d25f35e8c4e0d","cmd-2b213b9b6eac47b2","cmd-abcfe51a5b0747cd","cmd-af64ae8872864882","cmd-584d5f07d79d4b8e"]},{"id":"req-2-alias-object","requirement":"Ports 8080, 8443, 9000 expressed as a single named object referenced by the rule (not one rule per port)","status":"satisfied","summary":"A port-type alias 'node3_svc_ports' with content 8080/8443/9000 was created and a single filter rule referencing that alias name (not literal ports) was added to config.xml (confirmed via grep showing <port>node3_svc_ports</port>). After configctl filter reload, pfctl -s rules shows pf's own expansion of the alias into three port-specific pass lines sharing one identical rule label, which is the expected low-level rendering of a single alias-based rule rather than evidence of hand-written per-port rules.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3c8d10016eab438f","cmd-6b24ce4674984e59","cmd-e2d4e30913334e02","cmd-dea4745fba8e432b"]},{"id":"req-3-connectivity","requirement":"node2 can reach node3 on TCP 8080/8443/9000 while other ports remain blocked","status":"satisfied","summary":"From node2, TCP connections to node3 (10.50.2.2) on 8080, 8443, and 9000 all succeeded (exit=0), while a non-aliased port 9999 failed (exit=1). pf state table on node1 shows the three permitted flows. The same test set was re-run and passed after the mandatory node restarts, confirming persistence of the rule/alias across reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1a1c4c064eae43fc","cmd-e4dd8b1eaebe46e5","cmd-469f74b4b1eb4ac1","cmd-fc7879bdd64344eb"]},{"id":"req-4-restart-protocol","requirement":"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed on every managed node","status":"satisfied","summary":"All three nodes show a coherent pre-restart healthy baseline, a restart acknowledgement, a changed post-restart boot identity, and recovered service/config state: node1 (kern.boottime 16:31:25 -> 16:37:59, interfaces/pf-rules/forwarding re-verified), node2 (boot_id e4049301... -> 8ab9557b..., eth1 IP/route persisted, connectivity initially failed then recovered once node3's test listeners were restarted), node3 (boot_id 4b02f136... -> ad2bcc20..., eth1 IP/route recovered, listeners re-established, final cross-node port test passed). These transitions match the fixed after_executor boot-identity snapshot for all three nodes. One anomaly: the fixed before_prepare snapshot for node1 reports a much older boot time (Jun 15) than the executor's own pre-restart baseline reading (Sep 5 16:31:25) taken only minutes later, suggesting node1 underwent an unexplained boot-identity change prior to the executor's mandated restart sequence; this is noted as a limitation rather than a failure since the executor's own before/after restart pair is internally consistent and matches the fixed after_executor value.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a7495507b97b4613","cmd-db1958265e644a66","cmd-1e2d109ed4044cc5","cmd-584d5f07d79d4b8e","cmd-4b06a8e770e542bf","cmd-a44621c846b644ff","cmd-557784092d304a0b","cmd-1dffcc47686f4917","cmd-30f4785b00ef4b2c","cmd-ea41e39c317d498b","cmd-0baa250a0ded48f5","cmd-676c73d44e3042e4","cmd-f17c4c40e3ff41af","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node3:boot-identity","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity"]}],"confidence":0.82,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.75,"summary":"No destructive, broad, or security-weakening mutations were observed; state changes were bounded and traceable to the routing/rule/restart work. Minor residue was left behind: on node3, temp files /tmp/8080.log, /tmp/8443.log, /tmp/9000.log (from repeated nc listener tests) and /tmp/reboot.log were not cleaned up after final testing; node2 similarly retained /tmp/reboot.log. Config.xml backups (.bak1/.bak2) created before edits (cmd-2b213b9b6eac47b2, cmd-3c8d10016eab438f) were not shown to be removed, though this is a low-risk safety practice rather than harmful residue. An early manual `ifconfig`-based IP assignment on node1 (cmd-d40e672f742f4c46) was a working prerequisite step later superseded by the persistent config.xml-based interface definition (cmd-abcfe51a5b0747cd) rather than a wasted/unrelated mutation. Diagnostic steps (tcpdump captures, ad hoc pings, temporary `ip route add`) were read-only or fully superseded by persistent configuration and were cleaned up (tcpdump killed, nc processes pkilled) at least in part.","evidence_ids":["global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files","cmd-d40e672f742f4c46","cmd-2b213b9b6eac47b2","cmd-3c8d10016eab438f","cmd-624010d3